</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">E-mail </span><a href="mailto:star0874@vandals.uidaho.edu">star0874@vandals.uidaho.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern adaptive games require nuanced player understanding for personalization, yet current modeling approaches use simplified 5-10 category taxonomies that inadequately capture player diversity. Existing behavioral clustering cannot distinguish players with different motivations who exhibit similar actions. We present a systematic evaluation of multi-modal classification at realistic scales, examining how behavioral telemetry combined with semantic context performs when supporting 36 distinct player profiles.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">Using 19,413 gameplay sessions from an AI-controlled text-based Role Playing Game (RPG), we compared behavioral-only baselines against multi-modal approaches integrating action sequences with semantic descriptions. Traditional behavioral clustering achieved only 10% accuracy for 36-category classification, limited by semantic conflation where morally opposite actions produce identical statistical features. Our multi-modal LSTM architecture processing complete action-text pairs improved accuracy to 21%, revealing both the potential and limitations of non-conversational data.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Analysis by behavioral complexity revealed the core limitation: non-neutral profiles achieved 42% accuracy (15× above random baseline), matching established benchmarks, while neutral profiles’ performance dropped to 25% (still 9× above random). This performance gap exposes why behavioral-only approaches fail—identical actions like ’help the merchant’ cannot reveal whether players are genuinely neutral or employing strategic patience (players who appear neutral while waiting for advantageous moments to act). Without access to player reasoning, even multi-modal approaches struggle with this distinction, though the above-baseline performance confirms that a meaningful signal exists.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.4" class="ltx_p">With game-based prediction beyond 20 categories being virtually unexplored these findings establish empirical benchmarks for complex player modeling: behavioral data plateaus at <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>10% for 36 categories, while multi-modal integration enables 25% accuracy. For game designers, this provides concrete evidence that meaningful personality-based adaptation requires conversational interaction—our results demonstrate that predefined action choices alone cannot reliably capture player intent. Our systematic evaluation at a 36-category scale offers essential guidance for developing adaptive games that truly understand their players.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Player modeling Serious games LSTM Multi-modal classification Behavioral analysis adaptive games sequential modeling player profiling Game AI Conversational AI
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Player modeling in serious games enables personalized learning experiences through adaptive content and difficulty scaling. While existing research demonstrates success with simple player type classifications–with studies like Haizel et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> achieving over 90% accuracy for Five-Factor Model assessment and Denden et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> reporting high accuracy for two personality dimensions (extroversion and openness)–the approaches prove insufficient for serious games requiring nuanced adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current player modeling approaches face a complexity gap. Studies achieve strong results with simplified taxonomies: Denden et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> report high accuracy for binary personality dimensions, while 5-7 archetype systems maintain 60-75% accuracy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. However, accuracy drops to 40-55% for 16-type classifications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and game-based prediction beyond 20 categories remains virtually unexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We address this gap by implementing an LSTM-based approach to classify players into 36 distinct profiles combining moral alignments with core motivations. Adapting the alignment and motivation framework from Starace et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> for player classification, we implement an LSTM-based approach to classify players into 36 distinct profiles. We investigate whether sequential gameplay data contains sufficient signal for complex multi-dimensional classification. Our approach preserves temporal dependencies in narrative choices—information lost by traditional aggregation methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments with gameplay data from 19,413 text-based RPG sessions demonstrate that meaningful behavioral patterns exist even for highly complex classification tasks. While aggregated features achieve modest results above random chance, LSTM models processing full sequences achieve 21% accuracy for 36-class prediction (7.5× random baseline). These results establish both the potential and limitations of complex player profiling from sequential gameplay data, showing meaningful signal detection while highlighting the need for conversational approaches in serious games.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted previously, player classification declines rapidly as the number of profiles increases with almost no research being completed for more than 20 categories. This complexity ceiling stems from fundamental limitations in traditional approaches. Most studies rely on aggregated behavioral metrics—average actions per minute, total resources collected, completion rates—that discard temporal context distinguishing player personalities. Two players may collect identical resources but differ fundamentally in when and why they make these choices. For example, early-game resource hoarding suggests different motivations than late-game collection, yet aggregation treats these behaviors identically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Sequential modeling addresses these limitations by preserving temporal dependencies. LSTM networks analyzing gameplay sequences demonstrate 15-25% improvement over statistical summaries for predicting player outcomes and emotional states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Chulajata et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> show that bidirectional LSTMs capture strategic planning patterns invisible to aggregated metrics, while Ghosh et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> demonstrate superior emotion recognition by modeling the evolution of player stress throughout gaming sessions. Despite these advantages, no prior work applies sequential modeling to complex personality classification combining moral alignments with underlying motivations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Theory-driven approaches offer complementary advantages. Cowley and Charles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> introduce "Behavlets"—psychologically-grounded features derived from temperament theory that achieve 35% better accuracy than raw gameplay metrics. Their framework maps four temperament types (Logistical, Tactical, Strategic, Diplomatic) to observable patterns through systematic linkage between psychological traits and game mechanics. However, four broad categories lack the granularity needed for narrative-driven serious games where moral choices interact with player motivations. A player’s decision to help versus harm an NPC reveals alignment, while their resource management patterns indicate motivation—distinctions lost in temperament-only models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in adaptive serious games underscore the importance of comprehensive player modeling. Hare and Tang <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> survey adaptation methods, noting that effective personalization requires understanding both what players do and why they do it. Brezovsky et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> demonstrate that game-based learning environments can significantly improve mathematical learning outcomes through adaptive number knowledge training. Sajjadi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> identify twelve distinct aspects requiring adaptation in serious games—from learning style to anxiety levels—far exceeding the modeling capacity of existing player taxonomies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Adjacent domains demonstrate feasibility of high-dimensional classification. Social media personality prediction achieves high accuracy rates for complex personality frameworks, with studies reporting 86-88% accuracy for Big Five traits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> and 74-83% F1-scores for 16-type MBTI classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> using transformer models and massive datasets. These successes highlight both opportunities and challenges: games provide richer behavioral sequences but smaller datasets, with actions constrained by mechanics rather than free-form expression. A prior framework provided by Starace et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, proposed combining nine alignments with four motivations for 36 distinct profiles, but focused on NPC generation rather than player classification, this paper begins to tackle the issue of classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This 36-profile complexity represents unexplored territory in gameplay-based prediction. We address both the complexity gap in player taxonomy scale and the methodological gap in temporal modeling by combining sequential modeling’s temporal awareness with theory-driven features grounded in established moral and motivational frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Sequential Model Architecture and Analysis</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To establish performance benchmarks, we first evaluated traditional aggregated approaches on our dataset of 19,413 gameplay sessions. XGBoost processing behavioral features (action frequencies, normalized ratios, completion metrics) achieved only 10.4% accuracy for 36-category classification versus 2.8% random baseline, confirming fundamental limitations when behavioral patterns decouple from player intent. Sequential modeling preserves temporal dependencies lost in aggregation, enabling distinction between similar actions with different moral contexts. Thus, we developed LSTM architectures processing complete gameplay sequences with multi-modal features, to capture these temporal dependencies that aggregation discards.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Dataset and Architecture</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All 36 profile classes (9 alignments × 4 motivations), discussed by Starace et al.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, combine the 9 Dungeons &amp; Dragons alignments with 4 core motivations. Each profile class has at least 500 games, with sequence counts varying (4,437-7,911 per profile) as games contain different numbers of sequential decision points. Sequence-aware balancing under-samples to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>4,100 sequences per class, reducing imbalance from 1.78:1 to 1.15:1.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our initial LSTM model used 530 features per game decision, but 96.6% of these were BERT text embeddings—numerical representations of the game’s descriptions. Despite having all this information, the model achieved only 2.75% accuracy, no better than random guessing. The problem: the model focused on memorizing how rooms and actions were described rather than learning how players actually behaved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We tried compressing all 530 features into 256 dimensions, hoping the model would focus on what mattered, but this failed. So we completely redesigned our approach. Instead of letting text dominate, we created a balanced 176-feature representation where text takes up only 72.7% of the space. We expanded behavioral tracking from 10 to 48 features, capturing: how players transition between action types (15 features), what choices were available versus what was selected (12 features), temporal patterns like early-game versus late-game behavior (15 features), and movement patterns through the dungeon (6 features). This re-balancing improved accuracy to 22.77%—the model finally learned to recognize behavioral patterns rather than memorize text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Model Variants and Performance</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our best architecture employs bidirectional LSTM with multi-pooling—concatenating max and mean pooling captures both peak behavioral signals and average tendencies. This achieved 22.77% overall profile accuracy (8× random baseline) and 74.76% accuracy in predicting motivations.</p>
</div>
