<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">74.8%</span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">24.8% (20)</span>
<span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b" style="padding-top:2.5pt;padding-bottom:2.5pt;">Semantic Gap</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">LSTM architectures for 36-category player profile classification. Non-Neutral subset contains 16 profiles, Neutral-only contains 20 profiles. Multi-Pooling with 176 dimensions achieved best results, revealing a 17-point accuracy gap between behaviorally distinct (Non-Neutral) and ambiguous (Neutral) player types.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Investigating the Accuracy Ceiling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To understand why performance plateaued, we conducted systematic complexity reduction experiments using bi-directional LSTM classifiers with attention for binary classification:
Binary Classification: Lawful (Good, Neutral, Evil) vs Non-Lawful achieved 62-63% accuracy—barely above random for a binary task. This suggests alignment ambiguity persists even at the simplest level.
Three-way Classification: Lawful/Neutral/Chaotic classification reached only 44.7% (vs. 33.3% random baseline), confirming that the problem isn’t just category count but fundamental semantic ambiguity.
Neutral Correction: Attempting to correct the model’s tendency to over predict neutral alignments improved overall accuracy to 53.67% for alignment-only tasks, but profile accuracy remained limited.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Neutral Versus Non-Neutral</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Non-Neutral Profiles (16 categories: excluding True Neutral, Lawful Neutral, Chaotic Neutral) have clear behavioral signatures—good characters help others, evil characters cause harm. For these profiles the model achieved 42.13% accuracy, matching benchmarks for 16-class personality prediction from social media text.
Neutral Profiles (20 categories: all neutral alignments × motivations): These profiles lack distinctive moral indicators. The same action—"help the merchant"—could indicate genuine neutrality, strategic patience (waiting for an advantageous moment to act), or role-playing inconsistency. Accuracy dropped to 24.79%, still 5× above random. Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.4 Neutral Versus Non-Neutral ‣ 3 Sequential Model Architecture and Analysis ‣ A Systematic Evaluation of Multi-modal Approaches to Complex Player Profile Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> shows the confusion matrices for the non-neutral and neutral datasets.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.05624v1/confusion_matrices_20250614_230424.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:471/385;" width="471" height="385" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Confusion matrices for alignment prediction. Non-neutral model (left) shows clear diagonal pattern with 55.5% accuracy. Neutral model (right) exhibits strong bias toward neutral predictions, achieving only 32.8% accuracy.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3.4 Neutral Versus Non-Neutral ‣ 3 Sequential Model Architecture and Analysis ‣ A Systematic Evaluation of Multi-modal Approaches to Complex Player Profile Classification" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reveals the core issue: the neutral model defaults to predicting neutral alignments for ambiguous behaviors. While the non-neutral model achieves reasonable class separation, the neutral model’s confusion matrix shows heavy concentration in the neutral columns, indicating it cannot distinguish between genuine neutrality and other alignments exhibiting balanced behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Implications for Complex Player Modeling</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With many categories and only behavioral data, the models plateau at relatively low accuracies. Integration of temporal data leads to significantly higher accuracy, and the removal of neutral behaviors produces even higher accuracy. In these experiments specifically, behavioral data alone plateaus at <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>10% for 36 categories, temporal integration with action semantics reaches 22% accuracy, and non-neutral alignments reach 42% accuracy. The consistent under-performance across all complexity levels—from binary to 36-class—confirms that the limitation is semantic, not architectural.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The significantly higher classification rates observed in experiments on personality prediction using interactive social media data (e.g. extended conversations) strongly suggests that games requiring nuanced personality modeling or identification will need to incorporate more sustained interactions between the player and the classifying AI, such as interactive dialogue. Just as social media personality prediction leverages conversational text, adaptive games can infer player intent through natural dialogue and exploratory questions, capturing the subtle cues that distinguish genuine neutrality from strategic patience. The consistency of these limitations across multiple architectures and complexity levels confirms that current approaches face a fundamental ceiling rather than merely requiring better optimization.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings reveal a fundamental limitation in gameplay-based player modeling: behavioral sequences alone cannot capture the intent behind player actions. The 17-point accuracy gap between non-neutral (42%) and neutral (25%) profiles demonstrates that while distinctive moral behaviors can be classified at rates comparable to personality prediction benchmarks, ambiguous or balanced playstyles require a better understanding of player reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These empirical thresholds have direct implications for serious game design. For simple adaptation (5-10 profiles), behavioral telemetry suffices. Our baselines achieving 51.2% motivation accuracy suggest that broad player categories (Safety, Speed, Wanderlust, Wealth) remain detectable through aggregated metrics. For complex adaptation (20+ profiles), conversational interaction becomes essential. Games requiring nuanced understanding—distinguishing strategic patience from genuine neutrality—must incorporate dialogue systems and/or design mechanics that discourage neutral behavior patterns. This could manifest as reflective prompts (’Why did you help the merchant?’), character dialogue with NPCs asking about player motivations, journal systems where players document their reasoning, or voice interaction enabling natural language explanations during gameplay.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The semantic gap manifests most clearly in neutral alignments, where identical actions stem from different motivations. Consider the action “help the merchant”: for Lawful Good players, this reflects genuine altruism; for True Neutral players maintaining balance; for Chaotic Evil players, it might represent a strategic deception before betrayal. This finding parallels personality prediction research in other domains. Social media studies achieving 60-80% accuracy on Myers-Briggs types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> benefit from rich conversational data where users explain their thoughts and feelings. Game telemetry, constrained to predefined actions, lacks this explanatory dimension. Even our multi-modal approach—incorporating action semantics and context—cannot bridge this gap without access to player reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our results suggest current player modeling pipelines require fundamental restructuring. Rather than mining increasingly complex behavioral patterns, adaptive games should integrate conversational AI from the design phase. The recent availability of large language models makes this feasible—players can now engage in natural dialogue about their choices, providing the semantic context our models desperately need. This shift from behavioral to conversational player modeling aligns with broader trends in AI. Just as recommender systems evolved from click-through rates to understanding user intent through search queries, game AI must evolve from action tracking to understanding player reasoning through dialogue.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several limitations bound the interpretation of our findings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic Data Generation:</span> Our gameplay sessions were generated by LLMs simulating player behavior, which may not capture human inconsistencies, learning curves, and emotional responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Game Genre:</span> Experiments used a text-based dungeon crawler with discrete choices, limiting generalizability to other game genres with continuous actions or social mechanics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-Based Environment:</span> Our semantic features derived from textual descriptions, limiting applicability to visual games requiring 3-D interaction mapping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Profile Granularity:</span> Our static 36-profile taxonomy simplifies human personality and cannot capture contextual motivational shifts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work reveals a fundamental semantic gap in gameplay-based player modeling through the first systematic evaluation at 36-category complexity. Our core discovery—that non-neutral profiles achieve 42.1% accuracy while neutral profiles plateau at 24.8%—exposes why behavioral approaches fail: identical actions cannot reveal player intent. Multi-modal LSTM integration improves accuracy from 10% to 21% (7.5× baseline), but performance ceilings persist across all complexity levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These findings establish concrete design guidelines for adaptive games. For broad player categories (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math> 10 profiles), behavioral telemetry achieves practical accuracy thresholds around 50%. For complex personality modeling (20+ profiles), our 21% ceiling indicates that conversational interaction becomes essential—games requiring nuanced adaptation must integrate dialogue systems to capture player reasoning. Educational games distinguishing learning styles, therapeutic applications assessing emotional states, and social platforms modeling user personalities all exceed behavioral modeling capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our empirical benchmarks provide the game AI community with essential complexity thresholds: behavioral aggregation plateaus at <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>10%, multi-modal sequential modeling reaches <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>22%, and even optimal conditions cap at <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>42% for complex taxonomies. These results demonstrate that meaningful personality-based adaptation requires fundamentally different approaches than current behavioral clustering methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations open promising research directions. Conversational integration represents the most direct path forward—implementing real-time dialogue within gameplay and developing hybrid models that combine behavioral sequences with periodic dialogue to capture player reasoning with minimal disruption. The recent availability of large language models makes natural player-AI dialogue feasible, potentially bridging the semantic gap our models encountered.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Deception detection emerges as a critical parallel direction, enabling identification of players deliberately misleading classification systems—essential for competitive games where players may manipulate matchmaking or difficulty adjustment. This framework could extend beyond games to any adaptive system vulnerable to strategic user behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Additional opportunities include validating findings with human players to confirm whether the neutral/non-neutral performance gap persists, investigating cross-domain transfer from social media personality models, and developing interpretable player modeling that explains classifications to both developers and players. While our synthetic data revealed fundamental limitations, human studies may uncover behavioral signals invisible to current approaches, offering new pathways toward truly adaptive gaming experiences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Pt0.A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix 0.A </span>Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Pt0.A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Pt0.A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The work described herein was directly supported with computational infrastructure and expertise provided by the University of Idaho’s Research Computing and Data Services (RCDS) unit within the Institute for Interdisciplinary Data Sciences (IIDS).</p>
</div>
</section>
